# drag.conf.example — the line the operator writes to ARM the window-drag chord.
#
# 🚨 THIS FILE'S NAME IS THE SAFETY PROPERTY, NOT A NAMING CONVENTION.
# config/kitty.conf's last line is `globinclude drag-arm.d/*.conf`. This file is named
# `drag.conf.example`, which does NOT match `*.conf` — so even if config/drag-arm.d/ were ever
# symlinked into the live kitty config directory, or a sandbox pointed KITTY_CONFIG_DIRECTORY at
# this repo's config/ directly, kitty could never include it. The tracked tree is inert by
# CONSTRUCTION rather than by anyone remembering to keep it empty. Do not rename this file to
# end in `.conf`, and do not add a sibling that does: tests/kitty-drag-arm.bats fails the land if
# any `config/drag-arm.d/*.conf` becomes tracked, and that assertion is the whole proof that this
# wave's landed diff arms nothing.
#
# WHERE THE REAL FILE LIVES — and why it is NOT this one.
#   ~/.config/kitty/drag-arm.d/drag.conf      a REAL, UNTRACKED, initially EMPTY file,
#                                             created by scripts/kitty-setup.sh
# It is deliberately not a symlink back to this repo. If arming were an edit to a tracked file,
# a later land or a deploy-live fast-forward could clobber the operator's armed state, and the
# disarm rule below would be fighting git on every converge. A real untracked file is stable
# across every land.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-16, kitty 0.48.2: `globinclude` resolves relative to the path kitty is GIVEN,
# not to the resolved symlink target. With ~/.config/kitty/kitty.conf symlinked into the shared
# checkout, the drop-in kitty reads is ~/.config/kitty/drag-arm.d/*.conf — the live-side one.
# (Controlled: loading the same config via its real checkout path read the checkout-side drop-in
# instead, and emptying both fell back to kitty's built-in default.)
#
# TO ARM — one line, no restart, live in ~100ms via kitty's own __watch_conf__ child:
#     cat >> ~/.config/kitty/drag-arm.d/drag.conf   # then paste the uncommented line below
#
# 🚨 TO DISARM — EMPTY THE FILE, NEVER DELETE IT:
#     : > ~/.config/kitty/drag-arm.d/drag.conf
# A DELETION DOES NOT FIRE THE WATCHER (plan § 2.2 M2, measured). Emptying does, and withdraws
# the binding in ~3s with no restart, because the reload rebuilds opts.mousemap from a fresh
# empty dict (§ 2.2).
#
# 🚨 DO NOT PUT `window_title_bar_min_windows` IN THE LIVE DROP-IN. It was added there ad-hoc on
# 2026-09-16 (never from this file) to force a grabbable bar, and because this drop-in is
# globincluded LAST it silently made the ⌘⇧B toggle's OFF half UNREACHABLE: that half loads
# kitty.conf, whose final directive is the globinclude, which re-pinned the option on every load.
# The chord flipped its state file honestly and the bar never moved — measured, pane rows stayed
# 47 across three presses while `-o window_title_bar_min_windows=0` moved them to 48, which is how
# the pin was found. The operator then saw TWO titles: that pinned always-on bar, and the graphics
# overlay drawn at row 1 of the content area just beneath it.
# The general rule, which outlives this instance: a LAST-WINS drop-in is the wrong home for any
# option something else toggles — it does not merely set a default, it makes the other value
# unreachable, and the toggle reports success the whole time. Put a toggled option where the
# toggle owns it (kitty.conf for the resting value, config/kitty-title-on.conf for the raised
# one); keep this drop-in for bindings, which nothing else flips.
#
# ── THE ARMING LINE — COMMENTED OUT, AND THE CHORD IS A PLACEHOLDER ─────────────────────────
# 🚨 THE CHORD IS NOT CHOSEN YET. W4 is the hand-driven gate where the operator picks it, and it
# has not been run. `<CHORD>` below is a placeholder, NOT a default — do not guess one, and do
# not uncomment this line until W4 has ruled. Likewise `<ACTION>`: route A dispatches a kitten,
# route B a built-in `mouse_drag_window`, and which one ships is W4's other output.
#
# The two fixed parts, which W4 does not change and which are argued in the plan:
#   `press`, not `click` — promotion to a drag happens on MOTION, and a `click` is only
#                          synthesised on RELEASE, by which time the drag is over.
#   `grabbed,ungrabbed`  — Claude Code runs in essentially every pane and holds the mouse, so a
#                          binding that omits `grabbed` is dead in exactly the panes that matter.
#
# mouse_map <CHORD> press grabbed,ungrabbed <ACTION>
#
# For reference, the shape the plan sketches (§ W7) — still commented, still a placeholder:
# mouse_map cmd+shift+left press grabbed,ungrabbed <kitten under A · mouse_drag_window 2 under B>
